# Gigantes

## Lore

The Gigantes are the mutant giants, the strangest and most dreaded of their kind. Their bodies never settle: each one is born with its own mix of deformities and odd powers, and madness runs through the whole race. They are ignorant, quick to anger and fond of killing, with humans and elves their favorite prey, though other races and even other giants will do.

In the Baalgor Wastelands they are the wildest of the giants. They fight superbly and obey nobody, so most have been driven out of the giant villages or have wandered off on their own to hunt smaller folk, in a land that offers a fight in every direction.

**Alignment:** Any, but leans toward anarchist, miscreant and diabolic.

**Physical Appearance:** No two alike; most are monstrous and horrifying to look at.

**Size:** 15 to 20 feet tall (4.6 to 6 m); 14 feet plus 1D6 additional feet. **Weight:** 1,000 to 2,000 pounds (450 to 900 kg).

**Average Life Span:** 150 years; few pass 200.

**Habitat:** Anywhere, but mostly the Yin-Sloth Jungles, the Wastelands, the Old Kingdom Mountains and the Great Northern Wilderness.

**Enemies:** Titans and Rahu-Men above all, then Elves, Dwarves, humans, Wolfen and other canines, and most non-giants. In the Wastelands they are on especially bad terms with the Gromek.

**Allies:** Nimro, trolls, ogres, orcs and goblins. They often throw in with evil sorcerers, dragons, creatures of magic and the supernatural.

## GM Notes

The book says outright that Gigantes work best as villains run by the G.M. A player Gigante is a berserker with a rolled insanity who eats its enemies and may add a new insanity after any trauma; the hatred of Titans and Rahu-Men is shared by the whole race, but it is only the worst of the Gigantes who go for one the moment they see it. Settle with the player how much of that the group wants at the table. The four mutation rolls swing a great deal - from wings that may not fly to 1D6x10 extra S.D.C. - so make them in front of the G.M.
